      "metadata": {
        "id": "C3cl_NGq0Ox7"
      },
      "source": [
        "# **Section 2:** Python Fundamentals for GIS\n",
        "\n",
        "**Goal:** Build enough Python fluency to read, understand, and modify useful GIS scripts — not to become a Python programmer in 40 minutes.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "GXnPAbWTdc3J",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "GXnPAbWTdc3J"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 2.1 Core Python Building Blocks"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "Bi4k5k0qd3xZ",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Bi4k5k0qd3xZ"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Data Types\n",
        "Let's introduce the basic building blocks using things you already work with every day. These are the four basic data types you'll use constantly:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Type | Example | Notes |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| String (`str`) | `\"Public Works\"` | Text — always in quotes |\n",
        "| Integer (`int`) | `125430` | Whole numbers |\n",
        "| Float (`float`) | `125430.5` | Decimal numbers |\n",
        "| Boolean (`bool`) | `False` | `True` / `False` only |\n",
        "\n",
        "<br>"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "ezo06Dxb0Ox7",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ezo06Dxb0Ox7"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "department = \"Public Works\"\n",
        "parcel_count = 125430\n",
        "is_resolved = False\n",
        "\n",
        "print(department)\n",
        "print(parcel_count)\n",
        "print(is_resolved)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(type(department))\n",
        "print(type(parcel_count))\n",
        "print(type(is_resolved))\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "hFLm2GFt0Ox7",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "hFLm2GFt0Ox7"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Lists\n",
        "\n",
        "A **list** is an ordered collection — useful for field names, file paths, layers, categories, or items returned by an API.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "aFKdMP6Z0Ox7",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "aFKdMP6Z0Ox7"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "departments = [\n",
        "    \"Public Works\",\n",
        "    \"Planning\",\n",
        "    \"Code Enforcement\",\n",
        "    \"Parks & Recreation\",\n",
        "    \"Water Utilities\",\n",
        "    \"Animal Control\",\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(departments)\n",
        "print(len(departments))\n",
        "print(departments[0])       # first item\n",
        "print(departments[-1])      # last item\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "208LsQRa0Ox7",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "208LsQRa0Ox7"
      },
      "source": [
        "**Try it:** add `\"Animal Control\"` is already there — add one more department your organization has (e.g. `\"IT / GIS\"`) with `departments.append(...)` and print the list again. ...or try some other [List Methods]("https://www.w3schools.com/Python/python_ref_list.asp")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "JhHA0uLl0Ox8",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "JhHA0uLl0Ox8"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Your turn:\n",
        "\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "QrZvocTp0Ox8",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "QrZvocTp0Ox8"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Dictionaries\n",
        "\n",
        "This one deserves extra attention, because it's going to look *exactly* like what comes back from the ArcGIS API later this afternoon.\n",
        "\n",
        "A **dictionary** stores `key: value` pairs — think of it as a single record with named fields.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "ILO-4E1w0Ox8",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ILO-4E1w0Ox8"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "item = {\n",
        "    \"title\": \"Parcels\",\n",
        "    \"type\": \"Feature Service\",\n",
        "    \"owner\": \"gis_admin\",\n",
        "    \"id\": \"abc123\"\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "print(item[\"title\"])\n",
        "print(item[\"owner\"])\n",
        "print(item.keys())\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "aohVSsIN0Ox8",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "aohVSsIN0Ox8"
      },
      "source": [
        "> If you've worked with JSON or REST APIs, dictionaries are going to look very familiar. When we connect to ArcGIS later, every item, user, and layer comes back to us as a dictionary-like object with exactly this shape."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "v1jOKB4e0Ox8",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "v1jOKB4e0Ox8"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 2.2 Control Flow & Reusability"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "_QAl5D7cWxzq",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "_QAl5D7cWxzq"
      },
      "source": [
        "### `for` loops\n",
        "\n",
        "The pattern *\"for each thing, do something\"* shows up constantly in GIS work — iterating through layers, items, users, features."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "VoNehcZs0Ox8",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "VoNehcZs0Ox8"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "for department in departments:\n",
        "    print(department)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "HQtB69vv0Ox8",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "HQtB69vv0Ox8"
      },
      "source": [
        "### `if` / `else`\n",
        "\n",
        "Conditional logic lets your script make decisions.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "v_l6Dclw0Ox8",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "v_l6Dclw0Ox8"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "days_open = 45\n",
        "\n",
        "if days_open > 30:\n",
        "    print(\"Needs attention\")\n",
        "else:\n",
        "    print(\"Within target\")\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "5c_uk2wP0Ox8",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "5c_uk2wP0Ox8"
      },
      "source": [
        "> Spoiler alert: Later on, we'll use this pattern — `if` statements checking a condition — to flag stale GIS content and inactive user accounts across an entire organization."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "qR1ZAukW0Ox9",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "qR1ZAukW0Ox9"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Functions\n",
        "\n",
        "A function packages up logic you want to reuse, instead of retyping it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "7hLM6vm80Ox9",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "7hLM6vm80Ox9"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "def flag_old_item(days):\n",
        "    return days > 730\n",
        "\n",
        "print(flag_old_item(400))\n",
        "print(flag_old_item(900))\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "BaIs33no0Ox9",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "BaIs33no0Ox9"
      },
      "source": [
        "**Try it:** write a function `is_high_priority(request_type)` that returns `True` if `request_type` is `\"Water Main Break\"` or `\"Animal Bite Report\"`, and `False` otherwise."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "_gNHaq540Ox9",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "_gNHaq540Ox9"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Your turn:\n",
        "def is_high_priority(request_type):\n",
        "    ...\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "WYWqHSV60Ox9",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "WYWqHSV60Ox9"
      },
      "source": [
        "### A note on readable code\n",
        "\n",
        "Six months from now, someone else (maybe even you) will need to understand you script:\n",
        "- Use meaningful variable names (`days_open`, not `d`)\n",
        "- Add a comment when the *why* isn't obvious\n",
        "- Consistent formatting makes scripts easier to scan\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "12LPUyEK0Ox9",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "12LPUyEK0Ox9"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 2.3 Introduction to Pandas\n",
        "> Think of a DataFrame as a spreadsheet inside Python.\n",
        "\n",
        "Pandas is the tool we'll use for almost all tabular analysis today, and it's also how ArcGIS API results get turned into something you can analyze.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "J5RvJWn60Ox9",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "J5RvJWn60Ox9"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "import pandas as pd\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "_Q2gMsLE0Ox9",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "_Q2gMsLE0Ox9"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Import data from a CSV to a Pandas DataFrame\n",
        "df = pd.read_csv(\"311_requests.csv\")\n",
        "df.head()\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "0t5KtBfR0Ox-",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "0t5KtBfR0Ox-"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# View number of rows\n",
        "print(len(df))\n",
        "\n",
        "# View number of rows and column\n",
        "print(df.shape)      # (rows, columns)\n",
        "\n",
        "# View column names\n",
        "print(df.columns.tolist())\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "DXXOO9AV0Ox-",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "DXXOO9AV0Ox-"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Filtering: rows where department is Public Works\n",
        "df[df[\"department\"] == \"Public Works\"]\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "Zbx5koB10Ox-",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Zbx5koB10Ox-"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Counting values in a column\n",
        "df[\"request_type\"].value_counts()\n"
      ]
